<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- =============================================================================
     services/reference-service/pom.xml
     ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
     Purpose:
        Maven module descriptor for the reference-data bounded context, the sixth
        of nine modules in the com.carddemo:carddemo-services reactor. It names the
        module, binds it to the parent aggregator, and declares the exact runtime
        and test dependency set the module needs. The service it describes owns the
        PostgreSQL reference schema (transaction_types, transaction_categories,
        disclosure_groups, us_phone_area_codes, us_states, us_state_zip_prefixes)
        and serves transaction-type maintenance, disclosure-group interest-rate
        lookup, address lookup validation and date conversion, migrated from
        app/app-transaction-type-db2/cbl/COTRTLIC.cbl, COTRTUPC.cbl and
        COBTUPDT.cbl together with app/app-vsam-mq/cbl/CODATE01.cbl.

     Parameters (build inputs inherited from the parent, services/pom.xml):
        groupId com.carddemo and version 1.0.0-SNAPSHOT; the Java 21 release level
        and the UTF-8 source and reporting encodings; the imported dependency
        management of spring-boot-starter-parent 4.1.0,
        spring-cloud-aws-dependencies 4.1.0, software.amazon.awssdk bom 2.49.3 and
        testcontainers-bom 2.0.5; and the maven-checkstyle-plugin execution bound
        to the validate lifecycle phase that enforces Rule 1 on every build.

     Returns (build output):
        reference-service-1.0.0-SNAPSHOT.jar, an executable Spring Boot
        application carrying the Flyway migrations for the reference schema and the
        OpenAPI 3.1 contract, plus test reports written under
        services/reference-service/target/surefire-reports and
        services/reference-service/target/failsafe-reports.

     Errors (conditions under which this module fails the build):
        a Checkstyle violation at or above severity warning, which is the Rule 1
        documentation gate; a Surefire failure in any class matching *Test; a
        Failsafe failure in any class matching *RepositoryIT, most importantly the
        assertion that deleting a referenced transaction type is refused rather
        than cascaded; or an unresolvable coordinate, since every version consumed
        here is supplied by the parent rather than declared in this file.

     WHY (non-obvious design decisions):
        - Assumptions: XML 1.0 section 2.5 forbids two consecutive hyphen
          characters anywhere inside a comment. The rulers above therefore use
          equals and tilde runs where the sibling house header at
          .github/workflows/tests.yml line 3 uses a hyphen run, and no prose in
          this file contains consecutive hyphens or the comment close sequence.
          This is not a stylistic preference. A maintainer who "restores" a
          conventional hyphen ruler for visual consistency with tests.yml makes
          this file not well formed, and every Maven invocation that parses it then
          fails before a single goal runs.
        - Assumptions: Rule 1 names its four rationale categories as Alternatives
          Considered, Refactoring Rationale, Assumptions and Trade-offs, and the
          pre-existing house gate at tests/README.md line 544 to line 549 names the
          same four for every test, helper, mock and runner routine. The two
          authorities agree, and their plural spelling is the only accepted one.
          Every rationale label in this repository is written in that spelling and
          terminated by a colon; docs/CODE_DOCUMENTATION_STANDARD.md sets it out
          and enumerates the rejected shapes. A singular, bracketed, heading-style
          or dash-terminated variant is not an alternative spelling of a label, it
          is a label that a fixed-string search for the category will not find.
        - Assumptions: the parent owns all version management and the Checkstyle
          binding, so this file deliberately carries no properties block, no
          dependencyManagement, no version element on any dependency, and no
          repositories or pluginRepositories. Duplicating any of
          them would create a second source of truth that drifts silently. The
          build section at the end of this file is the one exception, and it exists
          because plugin MANAGEMENT is not plugin ACTIVATION: the Boot parent only
          pins spring-boot-maven-plugin's version, and services/pom.xml activates
          checkstyle, enforcer and failsafe but not that plugin, so a module needing
          an executable archive must declare the coordinate itself. The reasoning is
          recorded in full above that block rather than repeated here. The
          parent already overrides Spring Boot 4.1.0's own Flyway 12.4.0 and
          PostgreSQL 42.7.11 to 13.0.0 and 42.7.13, so a local copy of either
          number would quietly diverge the next time the parent moves.
     ============================================================================= -->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
    <artifactId>carddemo-services</artifactId>
    <version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <relativePath>../pom.xml</relativePath>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>reference-service</artifactId>

  <!-- Assumptions: the ECS Fargate task launches this artifact as an executable
       jar through the container entry point, so there is no servlet container to
       deploy into and war packaging would produce an artifact nothing consumes. -->
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <name>CardDemo Reference Service</name>
  <description>
    Reference-data bounded context for the CardDemo mainframe to AWS migration.
    Owns the PostgreSQL reference schema and serves transaction-type maintenance,
    disclosure-group interest-rate lookup, seeded address lookup validation and
    date conversion. Migrated from the Db2 transaction-type screens COTRTLIC and
    COTRTUPC, the batch reference update COBTUPDT, and the IBM MQ date-conversion
    flow CODATE01, with the date-edit rules of CSUTLDTC consumed from common-lib.
  </description>

  <dependencies>

    <!-- Refactoring Rationale: the CICS region was a single address space in which
         any program could reach any file, so the transaction-type screens and the
         interest batch touched the same VSAM and Db2 objects with nothing between
         them. The target decomposes that into bounded contexts, and common-lib is
         the ONLY reactor sibling this module is permitted to reach. Cross-service
         domain imports are forbidden by the ArchUnit rules that ship in
         common-lib, and a Maven dependency on a sibling service would defeat that
         boundary before a single import statement was written. batch-service and
         account-service both consume reference data, but over the published API or
         over the schema, never through compiled code.
         Assumptions: the parent aggregator manages the reactor version in its own
         dependencyManagement, which is what allows every module here to stay free
         of version elements. If that management is ever withdrawn the reactor
         fails fast with a missing version for com.carddemo:common-lib, and the fix
         belongs in services/pom.xml, the single source of truth for versions,
         rather than in this file. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
      <artifactId>common-lib</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Assumptions: common-lib declares spring-boot-starter-web,
         spring-boot-starter-validation, spring-boot-starter-security and
         spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server with optional set to true.
         Maven does not pass optional dependencies on to consumers, so all four are
         re-declared here. Omitting them compiles cleanly against common-lib and
         then fails at run time with a missing class, which is the most expensive
         place to discover the gap.
         Trade-offs: the alternative was a shared web starter module aggregating
         these four so that the services could inherit them in one line. It was
         rejected because batch-service is not a web application, and giving it a
         servlet stack and an OAuth2 resource server it never serves would enlarge
         both its image and its attack surface for no benefit. The accepted cost is
         that each web-facing service repeats these four declarations. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Assumptions: the six reference tables are mapped as JPA entities, and
         DIS-INT-RATE PIC S9(04)V99 at app/cpy/CVTRA02Y.cpy line 9 becomes
         NUMERIC(6,2) carried as a BigDecimal at scale 2. The persistence layer
         must therefore preserve exact fixed point rather than bind the rate to a
         floating point column. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Assumptions: two independent consumers poll the same health endpoint, the
         ALB target group and the container HEALTHCHECK, so the actuator is part of
         the deployment contract rather than a development convenience. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Assumptions: no compiled class in this module names a PostgreSQL type,
         because JPA and Flyway both reach the driver through JDBC at run time.
         Runtime scope keeps the driver off the compile classpath, so an accidental
         import of a vendor class fails the build instead of silently coupling the
         domain to one database product. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
      <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
      <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Assumptions: Spring Boot 4 split the one spring-boot-autoconfigure
         artifact into one autoconfiguration module per technology, so
         FlywayAutoConfiguration ships in
         org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-flyway and not with the framework
         core. This starter contributes that module along with spring-boot-jdbc;
         the two org.flywaydb coordinates below are the engine and the PostgreSQL
         dialect and activate nothing themselves. Without this starter every
         spring.flyway.* property is inert, neither V1__reference.sql nor
         V2__seed_reference.sql runs, and the service starts healthy against a
         reference schema with no disclosure_groups table and therefore no
         DEFAULT row, which is precisely the state the note below says hard abends
         the nightly interest run.
         Trade-offs: declared explicitly rather than expected as a transitive of
         spring-boot-starter-data-jpa, which brings spring-boot-jdbc but not
         spring-boot-flyway. One extra coordinate buys an activation that cannot
         vanish when an unrelated starter reorganises its own dependencies. The
         version comes from the parent, like every other coordinate here. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-flyway</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Assumptions: this module owns the reference schema, so it carries its own
         migrations rather than sharing a global set. One of them seeds the
         mandatory DEFAULT disclosure-group row. app/cbl/CBACT04C.cbl line 436 to
         line 439 falls back to that group when the group-specific read returns
         status 23, and line 455 to line 458 shows the fallback is not graceful:
         it displays ERROR READING DEFAULT DISCLOSURE GROUP and performs
         9999-ABEND-PROGRAM. A missing seed row hard abends the nightly interest
         run rather than degrading.
         Assumptions: the coordinate below is the STARTER, and naming it rather
         than flyway-core is what actually activates migration. Spring Boot 4 split
         its autoconfiguration into one module per technology, and
         FlywayAutoConfiguration now ships in spring-boot-flyway, which only this
         starter puts on the classpath. flyway-core alone resolves, compiles and
         starts, and then applies no migration at all, so the DEFAULT
         disclosure-group row the paragraph above depends on is never seeded and
         the nightly interest run abends for a reason that has nothing to do with
         the data.
         Refactoring Rationale: flyway-core is no longer declared directly. The
         starter brings it transitively at the version the parent's flyway.version
         property pins, so a direct declaration would add a second editable
         version site while contributing nothing to the classpath. -->
    <!-- Assumptions: Flyway 10 and later moved PostgreSQL support out of
         flyway-core into this companion artifact, and the starter above pulls no
         database module of its own. Without this coordinate the build resolves and
         compiles perfectly and then fails at run time on the first migration
         because no database type matches the JDBC url. This dependency is not
         optional and must never be pruned as apparently unused. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
      <artifactId>flyway-database-postgresql</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Assumptions: the OpenAPI 3.1 contract at
         src/main/resources/openapi/reference-api.yaml is the published interface
         that the browser client and the sibling services are written against, so
         the running service serves it directly. Leaving it a static file would let
         it drift away from the controllers with nothing detecting the drift. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springdoc</groupId>
      <artifactId>springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Refactoring Rationale: io.awspring.cloud:spring-cloud-aws-starter-sqs is
         WITHDRAWN from this module and its absence is deliberate rather than an
         omission. It was declared because app/app-vsam-mq/cbl/CODATE01.cbl is an
         IBM MQ request-and-reply front door as well as a synchronous one, and that
         queue flow was consumed here. It is not consumed here any more. The
         baseline drives BOTH of its inquiry programs from ONE request destination,
         DEFINE QLOCAL('CARDDEMO.REQUEST.QUEUE') at app/app-vsam-mq/README.md L53
         aliased to CICS as MQQUEUE(CARDREQ) at L71, and a queue admits exactly one
         OWNING consumer, because a receive hides the message from every other
         consumer rather than delivering a copy to each. The queue is therefore
         owned by com.carddemo.account.service.InquiryMessageListener, which
         dispatches on the request's four-character function code. What stays here
         is the date EVALUATION of app/cbl/CSUTLDTC.cbl on the synchronous route.
         Trade-offs: the starter is removed rather than left declared and unused.
         An unused messaging starter is not inert. Its auto-configuration builds a
         queue client as the context is constructed, which makes AWS_REGION a
         startup requirement for a module that addresses no queue, and it reads to a
         contributor as evidence of a message flow to go looking for. The accepted
         cost is that re-introducing a consumer here would have to restore this
         declaration, the spring.cloud.aws keys and a queue receive grant together;
         each of those is a decision, and the previous arrangement let all three be
         inherited silently. ReferenceServiceStructureTest asserts the annotation
         does not resolve from this module, so the withdrawal is measured rather
         than described. -->

    <!-- Assumptions: common-lib configures the Micrometer common tags but depends
         only on micrometer-core, which carries no exporter. The registry that
         actually publishes the scrape endpoint is a per-service choice, so it is
         declared by the service rather than pushed onto every consumer of the
         shared kernel. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
      <artifactId>micrometer-registry-prometheus</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Trade-offs: this starter pulls a large graph, JUnit 5 and Mockito and
         AssertJ alongside Hamcrest, JSONassert, JsonPath, XMLUnit and spring-test,
         in exchange for one declaration. The breadth is accepted rather than
         cherry-picking only the libraries this module actually calls, because test
         scope keeps every one of them out of the packaged image, so the price paid
         is build time rather than deployed bytes.
         Assumptions: the transcribed COTRTUPC and COBTUPDT validation branches are
         asserted one branch at a time, and the controllers through MockMvc slices,
         so no separate assertion or HTTP client library is declared here. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Refactoring Rationale: this declaration is REQUIRED and its absence was a
         real gap rather than a tidiness point. Spring Boot 4 moved the MVC test
         slice out of spring-boot-test-autoconfigure into its own starter, so
         spring-boot-starter-test above no longer puts WebMvcTest.class on the
         classpath at all. That was verified by listing the resolved
         spring-boot-test-autoconfigure jar, whose autoconfigure packages are json
         and jdbc only. Every controller suite in this module therefore had to
         hand-assemble a dispatcher, and a hand-assembled dispatcher cannot fail
         when the real component scan, the real message converter, the real
         advice bean or the real Jackson customisation is wrong, because none of
         them is present to be wrong.
         Assumptions: the version is managed by the Spring Boot parent, so no
         version element appears here; that is the same discipline every other
         Boot artifact in this file follows, and it is what keeps one upgrade of
         the parent moving the whole graph together.
         Alternatives Considered: keeping the hand-assembled dispatchers and
         adding a full @SpringBootTest instead. Rejected on both cost and
         precision, because a full context for this module starts a datasource, a
         Flyway migration and a resource-server decoder that reaches a discovery
         document, so a controller assertion would depend on four subsystems it
         is not about, and a failure in any of them would be reported as a
         boundary defect.
         Trade-offs: the slice deliberately excludes @Configuration classes, so
         the deployed security chain is still NOT installed by these suites. That
         is preserved on purpose and is not an oversight: with no chain, a 404
         means an unmounted address rather than a refused caller, which is a
         distinction several cases here depend on. The chain is asserted against
         its own installed authorization managers by the sibling
         com.carddemo.reference.config test package. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-webmvc-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Assumptions: every write path on this service is administrative and is
         guarded by the carddemo-admin group claim that common-lib derives from
         cognito:groups. A WebMvcTest slice has no identity provider of its own, so
         the add, edit and delete cases for transaction types cannot be exercised
         at all without a mock JWT carrying that claim. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.security</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-security-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Assumptions: testcontainers-bom 2.0.5, imported by the parent, manages
         exactly the artifact ids testcontainers, testcontainers-junit-jupiter and
         testcontainers-postgresql. Testcontainers 2.x renamed every module to that
         prefix, so the 1.x ids junit-jupiter and postgresql are no longer managed
         by the bom; naming them would leave the version unresolved and force a
         local version element that the parent is meant to own. Verified by reading
         the 63 managed entries in the bom itself rather than from documentation. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>testcontainers</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>testcontainers-junit-jupiter</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Refactoring Rationale: the single most important behavioural assertion in
         this module is that deleting a transaction type still referenced by a
         category is refused. app/app-transaction-type-db2/ddl/TRNTYCAT.ddl line 6
         to line 7 declares FOREIGN KEY TRC_TYPE_CODE referencing
         CARDDEMO.TRANSACTION_TYPE (TR_TYPE) ON DELETE RESTRICT, which Db2 reports
         as SQLCODE minus 532 and PostgreSQL as SQLSTATE 23503, surfacing as a
         Spring DataIntegrityViolationException that common-lib's
         GlobalExceptionHandler maps to HTTP 409. An in-memory database was the
         alternative and was rejected: it neither enforces ON DELETE RESTRICT
         identically nor runs Flyway's PostgreSQL dialect, so a green test there
         would prove the migration applied somewhere other than where it runs. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>testcontainers-postgresql</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

        <!--
          WHY : Refactoring Rationale: this module declared the Testcontainers engine and its JUnit
                integration but not the framework's Testcontainers support, and the repository
                integration tests its own test package charter enumerates could not run without it.
                @ServiceConnection lives here, and this module's src/test/resources/application-test.yml
                already DEPENDS on it: that file deliberately omits spring.flyway.user and
                spring.flyway.password and records, as a measured finding, that the container's
                @ServiceConnection is adapted into a Flyway connection-details bean so the container's
                own generated credential migrates the schema.
          WHY : Alternatives Considered: @DynamicPropertySource, which needs no extra artifact and which
                that test package's charter ruled for. Tried and MEASURED to fail: publishing only the
                three spring.datasource keys leaves spring.flyway.user bound to an unset SPRING_FLYWAY_USER
                placeholder, and every context load failed authenticating as the literal placeholder text.
                Publishing Flyway's credentials as well would have restated keys the same charter forbids a
                test class from restating, so the ruling could not be satisfied in either direction. The
                sibling ledger context resolves this the same way this module now does.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-testcontainers</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

    <!-- Assumptions: the interest rate this service serves is one of exactly two
         operands in app/cbl/CBACT04C.cbl line 464 to line 465, which computes
         WS-MONTHLY-INT as ( TRAN-CAT-BAL * DIS-INT-RATE ) / 1200, so a float or a
         double anywhere on the rate path would change cents on real balances.
         ArchUnit is what turns that prohibition, and the ban on importing another
         service's domain package, into a build failure rather than a note a
         reviewer might miss. The rule set is authored once in common-lib's test
         tree and delivered to this module as the test artifact declared below, so
         this engine is the assertion API the delivered rules need in order to
         load; test scope is not transitive, which is why it is named here. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.tngtech.archunit</groupId>
      <artifactId>archunit-junit5</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- WHY : Refactoring Rationale: the engine above was declared on its own, on
         the understanding that a rule class authored in common-lib's test tree
         reached this module by inheritance. Maven resolution passes a module's
         MAIN classes to its consumers and never its test classes, so nothing
         analysed com.carddemo.reference at all and the rate path prohibition
         cited above was unenforced here. common-lib now publishes that class as
         a test artifact narrowed to the architecture package, and the
         architecture-rules Surefire execution in services/pom.xml scans it into
         this module's own test run, where this module's compiled classes are the
         ones the rules see.
         Assumptions: the rules stay authored exactly once, in common-lib's
         architecture test package under the simple name LayeringRulesTest, which is
         the name the architecture-rules include pattern selects; this declaration
         adds delivery and not a copy. Version and scope are managed by the
         aggregator, so neither is stated here. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
      <artifactId>common-lib</artifactId>
      <type>test-jar</type>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- The four entries below are deliberate omissions. They are recorded next to
         the dependency list because an absent dependency has no element of its own
         to sit beside, and each is a choice a reader would otherwise have to
         re-litigate.
         Alternatives Considered: Lombok. Rejected because its generated accessors
         cannot carry the Javadoc that Rule 1 requires on every public member, so
         adopting it would trade an enforceable documentation gate for brevity.
         Java 21 record types with explicit constructors give the same brevity with
         members that can be documented.
         Alternatives Considered: MapStruct. Rejected because copybook to DTO
         mapping is not mechanical here. It drops FILLER, which is X(28) at
         app/cpy/CVTRA02Y.cpy line 10, X(08) at CVTRA03Y.cpy line 7 and X(04) at
         CVTRA04Y.cpy line 9, and elsewhere in the reactor the same layer applies
         masking and encryption and renames misspelled baseline fields. Each of
         those needs a justification at the mapping site and a generated mapper has
         nowhere to hold one. A second, practical objection is that MapStruct's
         most recent published release is a beta.
         Alternatives Considered: a resilience library. resilience4j-spring-boot3
         publishes against Spring Boot 3.x, and spring-retry is superseded. Spring
         Framework 7, which arrives through the Spring Boot 4.1.0 parent, moved
         retry into the core as @Retryable, @ConcurrencyLimit and a programmatic
         RetryTemplate, switched on by @EnableResilientMethods. Two details are
         easy to get wrong and are recorded here rather than rediscovered: the
         retry attribute is maxRetries, where total attempts are one plus its
         value, not maxAttempts, and the enabler is @EnableResilientMethods, not
         @EnableRetry. A circuit breaker is omitted as well, because the only
         synchronous hops are inside the VPC behind an internal load balancer with
         bounded timeouts, so a breaker would add a failure mode without removing
         one.
         Assumptions: spring-boot-starter-batch is absent by design; it belongs to
         batch-service. app/app-transaction-type-db2/cbl/COBTUPDT.cbl is labelled
         Layer: Business logic and declares SELECT TR-RECORD ASSIGN TO INPFILE with
         ORGANIZATION IS SEQUENTIAL, so its migration is a service method reading a
         sequential input, not a chunk-oriented job needing a job repository. -->

  </dependencies>

  <!-- =============================================================================
       Build
       ~~~~~
       WHY (non-obvious design decisions):
          - Assumptions: the Spring Boot parent only MANAGES spring-boot-maven-plugin
            in its pluginManagement; it does not run it. The reactor parent at
            services/pom.xml activates maven-checkstyle-plugin, maven-enforcer-plugin
            and maven-failsafe-plugin in its own build/plugins, which every module
            inherits, and it deliberately does NOT activate this one there, because
            common-lib is a library consumed on eight compile classpaths and must stay
            a plain archive: a consumer's compiler does not look inside BOOT-INF and
            would see none of its types. A module that needs an executable archive
            therefore has to name the coordinate itself, and this module does need
            one, because its container runtime stage launches a single self-contained
            jar. Without this block `mvn package` produces an ordinary jar with no
            Main-Class and no BOOT-INF layout, and the image's `java -jar` fails with
            "no main manifest attribute" at container start, from a build that
            reported success and a plan that showed nothing.
          - Trade-offs: nothing else is declared here, and the omissions are the same
            ones the header above records for the rest of the file. No version, since
            the parent chain pins it in lockstep with the framework; no executions
            block, since the Boot parent already binds the repackage goal and a local
            block would replace its configuration rather than extend it; no
            maven-checkstyle-plugin, maven-enforcer-plugin or maven-failsafe-plugin,
            since all three are inherited already active from services/pom.xml and
            re-declaring one would silently override whichever settings the
            re-declaration omitted, and weakening the documentation gate is a breach
            of the project's Explainability rule rather than a build preference.
       ============================================================================= -->
  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>

</project>
